1
00:00:00,213 --> 00:00:05,940
Walk into a coin, and it disappears. In the log, the player's
count increases by one.

2
00:00:06,101 --> 00:00:11,791
We'll build that interaction with native node graphs, then use
five copies along our woodland trail.

3
00:00:11,816 --> 00:00:15,921
Before adding a progress bar, let's make sure the number
underneath it is right.

4
00:00:21,680 --> 00:00:25,593
We'll place a coin and give it a detection area, create the data
it needs,

5
00:00:25,690 --> 00:00:31,102
connect the pickup graph, and test it in play. The finished
example counts from one to five.

6
00:00:31,221 --> 00:00:36,066
We'll also walk over a collected coin's old position and check
what happens when we retry.

7
00:00:36,861 --> 00:00:41,441
Start with a separate copy of the complete woodland. Keep the
previous lesson available.

8
00:00:41,608 --> 00:00:46,396
For the first few setup steps, I'm using a separate demonstration
copy with the same scenery.

9
00:00:46,472 --> 00:00:48,807
Then we'll return to the lesson's pickup graph.

10
00:00:48,947 --> 00:00:52,708
The finished lesson save and starter graph are included with the
companion files.

11
00:00:53,477 --> 00:01:00,334
In Place, expand Dynamic Units and open the gameplay category.
Search for coin and choose Adventure Coin.

12
00:01:00,431 --> 00:01:03,969
This is different from the Static Units we used for trees and
stones.

13
00:01:04,159 --> 00:01:07,859
We need an entity that can carry components and a node graph.

14
00:01:07,999 --> 00:01:10,943
A gold model alone doesn't define our counting rule.

15
00:01:11,595 --> 00:01:17,035
Place it above the trail and select its instance. Use Location to
put it where the character can reach it.

16
00:01:17,123 --> 00:01:22,295
My coordinates are one example. Check the visible coin rather than
copying numbers blindly.

17
00:01:22,413 --> 00:01:26,192
We want the player to pass through its detection area while
walking along the stones.

18
00:01:26,994 --> 00:01:32,972
Open the selected coin's Components tab. Choose Add Common
Component, then Collision Trigger.

19
00:01:33,027 --> 00:01:39,427
Open Advanced Editing and use the plus button to add a trigger.
This area detects another entity entering it.

20
00:01:39,494 --> 00:01:44,325
It's separate from a physical collider that blocks movement, and
it doesn't automatically award a point.

21
00:01:47,980 --> 00:01:53,898
Keep Initially Effective on. For Effective Target, select
Characters and clear Objects and Creations.

22
00:01:54,039 --> 00:01:57,545
Choose Sphere. This example uses a radius of one.

23
00:01:57,685 --> 00:02:02,836
That value controls the detection area around the coin; it isn't
the coin model's size.

24
00:02:03,061 --> 00:02:07,400
Keep the area centred on the coin and test whether the character
actually reaches it.

25
00:02:10,931 --> 00:02:16,402
We need a running total called Coins on the Player, and a Boolean
called PickedUp on each coin.

26
00:02:16,478 --> 00:02:21,949
The Player keeps the total as individual coins disappear. Each
coin keeps its own collected flag.

27
00:02:22,068 --> 00:02:26,138
Storing the total on the coin would be a problem when we destroy
that entity.

28
00:02:26,923 --> 00:02:31,348
Open Combat Preset, then Player, Player Template, and Default.

29
00:02:31,488 --> 00:02:38,391
Choose Edit Player, rather than Edit Character. In Components,
open Custom Variable and Advanced Editing.

30
00:02:38,416 --> 00:02:43,653
On Standard Variable, choose Integer and add Coins with a default
value of zero.

31
00:02:43,835 --> 00:02:46,831
Keep the spelling and capital C consistent in the graph.

32
00:02:47,580 --> 00:02:50,588
Coins is an Integer because we're counting whole items.

33
00:02:50,762 --> 00:02:55,742
Defining it here also makes it available to the native interface
binding we'll use next time.

34
00:02:55,839 --> 00:02:58,765
We're using a standard variable for this challenge session.

35
00:02:58,941 --> 00:03:03,847
We aren't setting up an account's permanent currency or an in-game
save system in this lesson.

36
00:03:06,495 --> 00:03:11,411
Return to the selected coin's Custom Variable component and open
Advanced Editing.

37
00:03:11,487 --> 00:03:15,871
Add PickedUp as Boolean, with the default set to No. No means
false here;

38
00:03:15,896 --> 00:03:19,361
Yes means true. A Boolean answers a two-way question.

39
00:03:19,480 --> 00:03:23,789
In our graph, that question is whether this particular coin has
already been collected.

40
00:03:35,538 --> 00:03:42,136
On the coin's Node Graph tab, choose Add Node Graph. Open
Miliastra Sandbox if you need to create one.

41
00:03:42,212 --> 00:03:46,232
In Server Node Graph Resource Explorer, choose Entity Node Graph.

42
00:03:46,308 --> 00:03:51,530
Right-click in the resource area and choose Create Node Graph.
Give it a recognisable name.

43
00:03:51,657 --> 00:03:55,449
This small creation demonstration is separate from the finished
pickup graph.

44
00:04:10,566 --> 00:04:13,860
In the graph editor, right-click to open the node library.

45
00:04:13,936 --> 00:04:18,249
Add When Entering Collision Trigger and Print String. Connect the
event's white

46
00:04:18,274 --> 00:04:23,894
execution output to Print String's execution input, then enter
coin entered as its text.

47
00:04:23,991 --> 00:04:29,142
Save the graph. This small chain checks an event before we add the
full pickup behaviour.

48
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:44,286
Back on the coin, use Add Node Graph to select the graph you
created, then save the scene.

49
00:04:44,533 --> 00:04:48,852
A graph in the resource explorer doesn't affect this coin until it
is mounted here.

50
00:04:49,013 --> 00:04:53,931
Check the name of your own graph. Its generated ID can differ from
the number in my example.

51
00:04:54,742 --> 00:05:01,492
Open Window, then Log in Miliastra Sandbox. Select the intended
server entity graph and confirm the selection.

52
00:05:01,611 --> 00:05:06,946
In a private test, move into the coin's sphere. Our minimal test
prints coin entered.

53
00:05:07,043 --> 00:05:10,466
This checks the trigger, mounting, and execution connection.

54
00:05:10,627 --> 00:05:14,050
Printing a message doesn't collect the coin or change our counter
yet.

55
00:05:14,889 --> 00:05:20,060
Now return to the lesson's pickup graph. It begins with that same
event and print test.

56
00:05:20,200 --> 00:05:26,386
We'll replace the direct execution connection with a condition, a
data update, a readback, and destruction.

57
00:05:26,580 --> 00:05:33,490
Keep a simple test in mind as the graph grows: entering one
uncollected coin should add exactly one.

58
00:05:34,299 --> 00:05:38,191
The white execution line tells the graph which action runs next.

59
00:05:38,352 --> 00:05:41,519
The round data pins carry values into those actions.

60
00:05:41,808 --> 00:05:47,081
Connecting a data value doesn't, by itself, tell an action when to
run. We'll use the event's

61
00:05:47,106 --> 00:05:51,842
Entering Entity for the character, and Trigger Entity for the coin
whose sphere was entered.

62
00:05:52,538 --> 00:05:58,893
Add Get Player Entity to Which the Character Belongs. Connect
Entering Entity to its Character Entity input.

63
00:05:58,958 --> 00:06:05,375
Its output is the affiliated Player Entity. We'll use that Player
as the target for Coins. The entering

64
00:06:05,400 --> 00:06:10,621
character and its Player are related entities, but they aren't
interchangeable targets for this counter.

65
00:06:11,385 --> 00:06:14,040
Add Get Custom Variable for the Trigger Entity.

66
00:06:14,116 --> 00:06:18,221
Enter PickedUp as the variable name and use Boolean for its value.

67
00:06:18,425 --> 00:06:24,223
Feed that value into the Condition of Double Branch. Connect the
event's execution output to the branch.

68
00:06:24,371 --> 00:06:29,000
If PickedUp is Yes, we leave that path without an award. We'll
continue from No.

69
00:06:30,316 --> 00:06:35,624
From the No execution output, add Set Custom Variable. Target
Trigger Entity,

70
00:06:35,649 --> 00:06:41,026
use the name PickedUp, and set its Boolean value to Yes. Do this
before increasing the total.

71
00:06:41,081 --> 00:06:45,542
The flag records that this coin has been handled, while the rest
of its pickup actions continue.

72
00:06:48,394 --> 00:06:54,446
Add another Get Custom Variable, this time targeting the
affiliated Player. Use Coins and Integer.

73
00:06:54,592 --> 00:07:00,284
Connect its value to Addition, and set the other Integer input to
one. This produces the next count.

74
00:07:00,309 --> 00:07:06,186
A query supplies a value when the execution chain needs it; it
isn't an extra execution step.

75
00:07:06,894 --> 00:07:10,678
Continue the execution line to Set Custom Variable for the Player.

76
00:07:10,734 --> 00:07:14,476
Enter Coins and use the Addition result as its Integer value.

77
00:07:14,658 --> 00:07:19,902
Read the targets carefully: PickedUp belongs to the coin, while
Coins belongs to the Player.

78
00:07:19,963 --> 00:07:23,826
A correctly spelled name on the wrong entity would still be the
wrong counter.

79
00:07:24,628 --> 00:07:30,302
After writing Coins, use a separate Get Custom Variable to read
the Player's current Coins value.

80
00:07:30,453 --> 00:07:36,116
Pass it through Data Type Conversion with String as the output
type, and connect that to Print String.

81
00:07:36,214 --> 00:07:41,664
We read back the stored count here. Don't print the earlier
addition again after changing the value it reads.

82
00:07:42,394 --> 00:07:47,886
Continue the execution line from Print String to Destroy Entity,
targeting Trigger Entity.

83
00:07:48,068 --> 00:07:51,528
The visible coin should now disappear after its count is stored.

84
00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:56,450
Check the chain from left to right: branch, mark PickedUp, write
Coins,

85
00:07:56,590 --> 00:08:01,708
print the stored value, then destroy that coin. Save the graph and
the editing scene.

86
00:08:02,446 --> 00:08:06,180
In a fresh private test, walk into the coin and stop.

87
00:08:10,383 --> 00:08:12,708
It disappears, and the log shows one.

88
00:08:13,340 --> 00:08:19,086
Walk back over its old position and check the log again. Our test
still has one pickup message.

89
00:08:19,181 --> 00:08:22,462
Now we can reuse this working coin along the rest of the trail.

90
00:08:24,475 --> 00:08:30,671
Once the single coin works, return to the editor. Right-click the
complete coin and choose Copy,

91
00:08:30,705 --> 00:08:35,928
then right-click empty ground and Paste. Move the new instance
before making another.

92
00:08:36,111 --> 00:08:40,899
Check that it has its own PickedUp flag, initially No, and the
same pickup graph.

93
00:08:41,017 --> 00:08:43,691
Repeat until there are five coins along your route.

94
00:08:48,375 --> 00:08:53,557
Save and start a fresh private test. Walk along the trail and
collect the coins in order.

95
00:08:59,361 --> 00:09:05,992
In this run, each coin disappears, and the same log records one,
two, three, four,

96
00:09:06,017 --> 00:09:11,546
and five. That visible sequence is more useful than assuming the
copies work because they look alike.

97
00:09:15,447 --> 00:09:19,734
Choose Retry Challenge and try the first coin again. The coins
reappear.

98
00:09:24,383 --> 00:09:27,579
After the new Stage Start, the next pickup prints one.

99
00:09:28,256 --> 00:09:32,510
This checks the new session's starting value, rather than
continuing from five.

100
00:09:32,736 --> 00:09:38,810
If your counter carries over unexpectedly, check which variable
type and reset path your own project is using.

101
00:09:39,588 --> 00:09:45,912
Before moving on, point to the event, the coin's flag, the
Player's total, and the final destruction step.

102
00:09:46,031 --> 00:09:52,938
Explain why the two variables have different owners. Then collect
one coin, revisit its position, and retry.

103
00:09:52,999 --> 00:09:58,399
You should be able to recognise the intended result without
matching my object coordinates or graph ID.

104
00:09:59,194 --> 00:10:04,203
Keep the five-coin lesson save. In the next episode, we'll build a
native progress bar

105
00:10:04,228 --> 00:10:09,542
that reads this real Player variable. The counter is now tested
underneath the interface,

106
00:10:09,618 --> 00:10:14,316
so we can concentrate on showing its value clearly while the
player moves through the woodland.
